<h2>What is a normal cortisol level?</h2>
<p><strong>A cortisol result needs a sample type, collection time, unit and laboratory reference interval.</strong> A generic online chart cannot classify every person's result. Cortisol changes through the day, and a serum result cannot be compared directly with a saliva or urine result.</p>
<h2>Blood cortisol reference ranges</h2>
<p>These are examples from named sources, not interchangeable diagnostic cutoffs. Use the reference interval on your own report.</p>
<table><caption>Examples of published serum cortisol reference intervals</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Source and group</th><th scope="col">Timing</th><th scope="col">Range</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.mayocliniclabs.com/test-catalog/Overview/8545">Mayo Clinic Laboratories CORT, adults 18+</a></td><td>a.m.</td><td>7–25 mcg/dL</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mayo CORT, adults 18+</td><td>p.m.</td><td>2–14 mcg/dL</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003693.htm">MedlinePlus blood-test example</a></td><td>8 a.m.</td><td>5–25 mcg/dL (138–690 nmol/L)</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The difference between these examples shows why “normal cortisol” is not one universal number. Tests performed after a suppression or stimulation medicine have their own interpretation.</p>
<h2>Normal cortisol levels by age</h2>
<p>Mayo's CORT assay lists separate pediatric intervals and an adult category beginning at 18. It does not provide a different target for every adult decade. Do not invent a goal for your 30s, 40s or 60s by extrapolating a social-media chart. For a child's result, use the age-specific interval from the testing laboratory and the child's clinician.</p>
<h2>Time of day and the daily curve</h2>
<p>Blood cortisol generally follows a daily rhythm, with higher morning and lower late-evening levels. A single sample is one observation, not a complete day of hormone output. Record when the sample was collected and follow the instructions for that test.</p>
<h2>Sex, pregnancy and medication</h2>
<p>Pregnancy and estrogen-containing medication can affect total serum cortisol and its binding proteins. Mayo's assay documentation describes these and other interpretation cautions. An online gender-based correction is not a substitute for that context. Tell your clinician what medicines and supplements you use; do not stop prescribed medication to change a result without medical guidance.</p>
<h2>Saliva and urine are different measurements</h2>
<p>In a clinical evaluation, the question determines the test. <a href="https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/cushings-syndrome">NIDDK describes late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urinary free cortisol and dexamethasone suppression testing</a> among the approaches used when Cushing's syndrome is suspected. A four-point wellness panel is not automatically the right next step for nonspecific fatigue.</p>
<h2>What to do with an unexpected result</h2>
<ul><li>Check the sample type, units, collection time and printed reference interval.</li><li>Ask the ordering clinician how your symptoms, medication and test protocol affect interpretation.</li><li>Ask whether follow-up testing is needed. A reference-range flag is not a diagnosis by itself.</li></ul>
<h2>Can Cortisol+ tell me whether my level is normal?</h2>
<p>No. Cortisol+ displays wearable stress and recovery signals and a wellness score. It cannot determine whether blood, saliva or urine cortisol is normal. A 0–100 app score must never be substituted into a laboratory chart.</p>
